<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Mount Lebanon Double Qaimaqamate</h1><p class="st-meta">1842 CE – 1861 CE</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A failed division of Mount Lebanon into Christian and Druze districts, which ended in the civil war of 1860.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the end of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mount-lebanon-emirate">Mount Lebanon Emirate</a> in 1842 and fighting between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronites</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the European powers agreed to divide the mountain along the Beirut-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> road. A Maronite qaimaqam governed the north and a Druze qaimaqam the south, both under the Ottoman governor in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beirut">Beirut</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The division ignored the many mixed villages, and the 1845 rules of Shakib Efendi added councils representing each community, which did not end the tensions. In 1858 Maronite peasants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keserwan">Kisrawan</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanyus-shahin">Tanyus Shahin</a> rose against their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazen-family">Khazin</a> overlords and expelled them, a social revolt that alarmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> lords in the south.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1860 a civil war broke out in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> fighters killed thousands of Christians, followed by a massacre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> sent troops, and an international commission created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mount-lebanon-mutasarrifate">Mount Lebanon Mutasarrifate</a> in 1861.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1842 CE – 1861 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.36%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1842 CE – 1861 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Haydar Ismail Abu'l-Lama, Ahmad Arslan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maronite Christianity, Druze</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Double Qaimaqamate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1852&amp;focus=mount-lebanon-double-qaimaqamate" aria-label="Mount Lebanon Double Qaimaqamate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5597 933 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mount Lebanon Double Qaimaqamate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5912 1207h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5917 1189h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5917 1189h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Lebanon. <a href="/atlas?year=1852&amp;focus=mount-lebanon-double-qaimaqamate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
